{"name":"Gold Spike","hex":"#907047","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"gold","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A heavy yellow elemental metal of great value, with atomic number 79.","A coin or coinage made of this material, or supposedly so."]},{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["☉ (alchemy)"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gold"},{"term":"spike","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A sort of very large nail.","A piece of pointed metal etc. set with points upward or outward."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To fasten with spikes, or long, large nails.","To set or furnish with spikes."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/spike"}],"entries":[{"title":"Gold Spike (property)","blocks":[{"text":"Gold Spike is a bar, lounge, residential building, and former boutique 112-room, seven floor hotel. It is connected with the Oasis at the Gold Spike, a 50-room three floor hotel located in downtown Las Vegas. It was owned by entrepreneur Tony Hsieh and his Downtown Project, having bought it from The Siegel Group; and the casino was operated by Golden Gaming."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Goldspike_2009.jpg/330px-Goldspike_2009.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold_Spike_(property)","wikidata":"Q5578861"},{"title":"Golden spike","blocks":[{"text":"The golden spike is the ceremonial 17.6-karat gold spike driven to mark the completion of the first transcontinental railroad across the United States. It was driven by Leland Stanford to connect the Central Pacific Railroad from Sacramento and the Union Pacific Railroad from Omaha on May 10, 1869, at Promontory Summit, Utah Territory. The spike is now displayed in the Cantor Arts Center at Stanford University."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/The-Golden-Spike-7Oct2012.jpg/330px-The-Golden-Spike-7Oct2012.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Golden_spike","wikidata":"Q4384193"},{"title":"Gold","blocks":[{"text":"Gold is a chemical element; its chemical symbol is Au and atomic number 79. In its pure form, it is a bright-metallic-yellow, dense, soft, malleable, and ductile metal. Chemically, gold is a transition metal, a group 11 element, and one of the noble metals. It is one of the least reactive chemical elements, being the second lowest in the reactivity series, with only platinum ranked as less reactive. Gold is solid under standard conditions."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Gold-crystals.jpg/330px-Gold-crystals.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold","wikidata":"Q897"},{"title":"Gold (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Gold, also called golden, is a color tone resembling the chemical element gold."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg/330px-GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold_(color)","wikidata":"Q208045"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Gold_Spike_bar_demolishing.jpg/500px-Gold_Spike_bar_demolishing.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Gold_Spike_bar_demolishing.jpg/500px-Gold_Spike_bar_demolishing.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Remodeling of the bar after its purchase by Hsieh","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGold_Spike_bar_demolishing.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/%22The_Driving_of_the_Last_Spike%22_painted_by_Thomas_Hill.jpg/500px-%22The_Driving_of_the_Last_Spike%22_painted_by_Thomas_Hill.jpg?utm